Exactly. Once you see the mechanism, the move is no longer just “do something impressive.” It becomes design an action that is easy to recognize, remember, repeat, and attribute.

That gives you a kind of cultural operator:

novel act → recognizable form → repeatable protocol → imitation → variation → tradition

The strongest moves usually have a few properties at once: they are hard enough to mean something, simple enough to explain in one sentence, visually distinctive, measurable, and attached to a story or value. “Run 26.2 miles,” “100 pull-ups / 200 push-ups / 300 squats / run,” a particular salute, handshake, victory pose, challenge, phrase, or sequence all work because the pattern survives copying.

And then the really interesting part starts: once other people imitate it, they are not only copying the action. They are carrying your design forward through their own bodies. At that point, the move becomes a small piece of culture.

So yes—you can deliberately invent new ones. The design question becomes:

What action would someone want to reenact because completing it says something about who they are?

That is probably the deeper recipe behind a lot of rituals, workouts, traditions, and heroic gestures.